WEBVTT

NOTE SA1 Topic 3.2: Zones and Records. Timings are estimates at 150 words a minute; re-sync after recording.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
Welcome to Topic 3.

2
00:00:01.850 --> 00:00:03.450
2, Zones and Records.

3
00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:07.300
In this topic you build the practice
namespace lakeshore.

4
00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:08.750
test.

5
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:14.600
You will compare primary, secondary and
stub zones, and meet the record types you

6
00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:16.050
use every day.

7
00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:22.700
You will create forward and reverse zones
with the New Zone Wizard, and add A, PTR,

8
00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:26.950
CNAME and MX records in DNS Manager and in
PowerShell.

9
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:33.600
Then you will copy the zone to SRV-CORE,
and then follow one change from SRV01 to

10
00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:35.850
SRV-CORE through the serial number.

11
00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:42.900
A zone is the part of the DNS namespace
that one server answers for, with all its

12
00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:44.350
records.

13
00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:47.800
The primary zone is the writable copy:
lakeshore.

14
00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:49.250
test on SRV01.

15
00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:55.100
A secondary zone is a read-only copy
pulled by zone transfer, for a spare

16
00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:56.950
server and shared load.

17
00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:01.200
A stub zone only lists the servers for a
zone.

18
00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:03.850
Our zones are file-backed text files.

19
00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:08.500
Active Directory-integrated zones need a
domain controller, so they wait for

20
00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:09.950
Learning Plan 4.

21
00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:15.800
Every record has a name, a type, a time to
live and its data.

22
00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:21.650
The SOA record, start of authority, holds
the primary server, the serial number and

23
00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:23.100
the timers.

24
00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:26.950
NS names a server that answers for the
zone.

25
00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:29.200
Both come with the zone.

26
00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:33.050
A maps a name to IPv4; AAAA to IPv6.

27
00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:36.500
A CNAME is an alias for another name.

28
00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:38.750
MX names the mail server.

29
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:42.200
PTR maps an address back to a name.

30
00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:44.850
SRV records arrive with Active Directory.

31
00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:46.700
Each lab starts fresh.

32
00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:50.550
On SRV01, run Install-WindowsFeature, dash
Name DNS, dash IncludeManagementTools.

33
00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:56.400
One command adds the DNS Server role, DNS
Manager and the PowerShell module, and

34
00:01:56.650 --> 00:01:59.050
opens port 53 in the firewall.

35
00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:00.900
No restart is needed.

36
00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:06.750
Get-Service DNS shows the DNS Server
service running, but it has no zones of

37
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:08.200
its own yet.

38
00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:10.850
Now open Server Manager, Tools, DNS.

39
00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:15.900
Expand SRV01, and you see Forward Lookup
Zones and Reverse Lookup Zones.

40
00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:18.950
That is where your zones will go.

41
00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:22.400
Right-click Forward Lookup Zones and
choose New Zone.

42
00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:27.050
After the welcome page, the wizard asks
for the zone type.

43
00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:33.300
Primary zone creates the copy you can
update on this server, which is what we

44
00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:34.750
want for lakeshore.

45
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:36.200
test.

46
00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:41.250
Secondary zone copies a zone from another
server; SRV-CORE uses that later.

47
00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:44.300
Stub zone holds only name server records.

48
00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:50.550
Store the zone in Active Directory is
greyed out, because SRV01 is not a domain

49
00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:52.000
controller.

50
00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:55.050
So this zone lives in a file.

51
00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:57.700
Next, type the zone name, lakeshore.

52
00:02:57.950 --> 00:02:59.150
test.

53
00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:04.600
The Zone File page suggests the zone name
plus dot dns, so lakeshore.

54
00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:06.050
test.

55
00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:09.100
dns, in the Windows System32 dns folder.

56
00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:10.550
Keep that default.

57
00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:12.800
Then the Dynamic Update page.

58
00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:18.250
Secure updates need an Active Directory
zone, and nonsecure updates would let any

59
00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:19.700
computer change records.

60
00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:22.750
So select Do not allow dynamic updates.

61
00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:26.600
Nothing registers itself; you create every
record by hand.

62
00:03:26.850 --> 00:03:32.850
Finish the wizard, and the zone appears
with its SOA and NS records already in

63
00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:34.300
place.

64
00:03:34.550 --> 00:03:38.550
A reverse lookup turns an address back
into a name.

65
00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:44.000
DNS names read from specific to general,
but addresses read from general to

66
00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:47.450
specific, so the numbers are reversed
under in-addr.

67
00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:48.900
arpa.

68
00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:50.350
The address 192.

69
00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:51.800
168.

70
00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:53.250
50.

71
00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:54.700
20 becomes 20.

72
00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:56.150
50.

73
00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:57.600
168.

74
00:03:57.850 --> 00:03:59.050
192.

75
00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:00.500
in-addr.

76
00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:01.950
arpa.

77
00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:03.800
The zone is 50.

78
00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:05.250
168.

79
00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:06.700
192.

80
00:04:06.950 --> 00:04:08.150
in-addr.

81
00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:12.800
arpa, and inside it the PTR record is
simply named 20.

82
00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:17.850
In the wizard, right-click Reverse Lookup
Zones, choose Primary zone and IPv4.

83
00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:21.700
Then type the Network ID in normal order,
192.

84
00:04:21.950 --> 00:04:23.150
168.

85
00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:24.600
50.

86
00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:28.850
The wizard fills in the reversed zone name
for you.

87
00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:32.300
Here are the same two zones in PowerShell.

88
00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:34.550
Add-DnsServerPrimaryZone with dash Name
lakeshore.

89
00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:38.800
test, dash ZoneFile, and dash
DynamicUpdate None creates the forward

90
00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:40.250
zone.

91
00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:43.700
For the reverse zone, use dash NetworkId
192.

92
00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:45.150
168.

93
00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:46.600
50.

94
00:04:46.850 --> 00:04:51.250
0 slash 24, and the cmdlet works out the
reversed name.

95
00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:56.300
Get-DnsServerZone lists both; your list
may also show a zone called TrustAnchors,

96
00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:57.750
which is normal.

97
00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:03.200
IsDsIntegrated says False, so they are
file-backed, with one plain text file per

98
00:05:03.450 --> 00:05:04.650
zone.

99
00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:07.300
To add a host, right-click lakeshore.

100
00:05:07.550 --> 00:05:09.550
test and choose New Host.

101
00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:15.000
Type only the name, srv01, and the full
name fills in by itself.

102
00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:17.250
Type the IP address, 192.

103
00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:18.700
168.

104
00:05:18.950 --> 00:05:20.150
50.

105
00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:21.600
20.

106
00:05:21.850 --> 00:05:25.850
Now the important check box: Create
associated pointer, PTR, record.

107
00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:32.100
It also adds record 20 to the reverse
zone, so one dialog makes two records.

108
00:05:32.350 --> 00:05:37.150
That only works if the reverse zone
exists, so create it first.

109
00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:38.600
Select Add Host.

110
00:05:38.850 --> 00:05:43.650
The dialog stays open for the next host,
and Done closes it.

111
00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:45.500
Two more record types.

112
00:05:45.750 --> 00:05:48.950
Right-click the zone and choose New Alias,
CNAME.

113
00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:53.200
The alias is www, and the target host is
srv01.

114
00:05:53.450 --> 00:05:54.650
lakeshore.

115
00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:56.100
test.

116
00:05:56.350 --> 00:05:59.950
The target is a name, never an IP address.

117
00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:02.200
Next, New Mail Exchanger, MX.

118
00:06:02.450 --> 00:06:07.250
Leave Host or child domain blank, so the
record is for lakeshore.

119
00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:08.700
test itself.

120
00:06:08.950 --> 00:06:10.950
The mail server is mail.

121
00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:12.400
lakeshore.

122
00:06:12.650 --> 00:06:16.250
test, with priority 10; lower numbers are
tried first.

123
00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:19.700
The mail server needs its own A record.

124
00:06:19.950 --> 00:06:22.350
In the pod it is 192.

125
00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:23.800
168.

126
00:06:24.050 --> 00:06:25.250
50.

127
00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:27.100
25, an unused address.

128
00:06:27.350 --> 00:06:28.950
DNS does not check.

129
00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:33.600
In PowerShell, first store the two zone
names in short variables.

130
00:06:33.850 --> 00:06:38.250
Add-DnsServerResourceRecordA with a name,
an address and the dash CreatePtr switch

131
00:06:38.500 --> 00:06:43.700
makes the A and PTR records in one step,
like the check box.

132
00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:48.350
Or use two steps: the A record, then
Add-DnsServerResourceRecordPtr with the

133
00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:51.000
name 30 in the reverse zone.

134
00:06:51.250 --> 00:06:54.850
The CNAME cmdlet uses dash HostNameAlias
for the target.

135
00:06:55.100 --> 00:07:01.500
The MX cmdlet uses the name dot, meaning
the zone itself, plus the mail server and

136
00:07:01.750 --> 00:07:02.950
its preference number.

137
00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:07.200
Before SRV-CORE can copy the zone, SRV01
must allow it.

138
00:07:07.450 --> 00:07:09.450
Open the properties of lakeshore.

139
00:07:09.700 --> 00:07:12.500
test and select the Zone Transfers tab.

140
00:07:12.750 --> 00:07:19.150
A zone transfer hands over every name and
address in the zone, so be careful who

141
00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:20.600
gets it.

142
00:07:20.850 --> 00:07:24.850
Select Allow zone transfers, then Only to
the following servers.

143
00:07:25.100 --> 00:07:27.100
Use Edit to add 192.

144
00:07:27.350 --> 00:07:28.550
168.

145
00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:30.000
50.

146
00:07:30.250 --> 00:07:31.850
21, which is SRV-CORE.

147
00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:36.900
Then select Notify, choose The following
servers, and add the same address.

148
00:07:37.150 --> 00:07:40.750
In PowerShell, Set-DnsServerPrimaryZone
does the same with dash SecureSecondaries

149
00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:43.000
TransferToSecureServers and the Notify
parameters.

150
00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:47.250
SRV-CORE has no DNS Manager, so this is
all PowerShell.

151
00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:49.100
Use SConfig option fifteen.

152
00:07:49.350 --> 00:07:53.350
Install the DNS role with the same command
as before.

153
00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:56.400
Then run Add-DnsServerSecondaryZone with
the name lakeshore.

154
00:07:56.650 --> 00:08:00.250
test, a zone file name, and dash
MasterServers 192.

155
00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:01.700
168.

156
00:08:01.950 --> 00:08:03.150
50.

157
00:08:03.400 --> 00:08:04.600
20.

158
00:08:04.850 --> 00:08:08.050
The master is the server it copies from.

159
00:08:08.300 --> 00:08:10.300
Get-DnsServerZone now shows ZoneType
Secondary.

160
00:08:10.550 --> 00:08:13.750
After a short wait,
Get-DnsServerResourceRecord lists the SOA,

161
00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:17.200
the A records, the CNAME and the rest.

162
00:08:17.450 --> 00:08:19.850
The data arrived by zone transfer.

163
00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:21.700
This copy is read-only.

164
00:08:21.950 --> 00:08:24.350
How does a change reach SRV-CORE?

165
00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:27.400
Look at the Start of Authority tab.

166
00:08:27.650 --> 00:08:34.450
The serial number goes up by one with
every change on SRV01, say from 10 to 11.

167
00:08:34.700 --> 00:08:37.100
Because of Notify, SRV01 tells 192.

168
00:08:37.350 --> 00:08:38.550
168.

169
00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:40.000
50.

170
00:08:40.250 --> 00:08:42.250
21 that the zone changed.

171
00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:48.900
SRV-CORE asks for the SOA, sees 11 is
higher than its 10, and pulls the changes.

172
00:08:49.150 --> 00:08:54.750
If a notify gets lost, SRV-CORE checks
anyway at the refresh interval, 15 minutes

173
00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:56.200
by default.

174
00:08:56.450 --> 00:08:58.850
Start-DnsServerZoneTransfer forces a check
at once.

175
00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:00.300
Now test.

176
00:09:00.550 --> 00:09:04.550
Resolve-DnsName with dash Server picks
which DNS server to ask.

177
00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:06.400
Ask SRV-CORE, at 192.

178
00:09:06.650 --> 00:09:07.850
168.

179
00:09:08.100 --> 00:09:09.300
50.

180
00:09:09.550 --> 00:09:13.150
21, for srv01, and you get the A record.

181
00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:20.200
Ask for www, and you get two answers: the
CNAME, then the A record it leads to.

182
00:09:20.450 --> 00:09:25.250
With dash Type MX you see the mail server
and preference 10.

183
00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:27.900
For a PTR lookup of 192.

184
00:09:28.150 --> 00:09:29.350
168.

185
00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:30.800
50.

186
00:09:31.050 --> 00:09:32.250
20, ask SRV01.

187
00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:38.900
SRV-CORE has a copy of the forward zone
only, so in our pod it simply cannot

188
00:09:39.150 --> 00:09:40.350
answer that one.

189
00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:41.800
Four mistakes.

190
00:09:42.050 --> 00:09:46.050
One: checking the PTR box before the
reverse zone exists.

191
00:09:46.300 --> 00:09:49.900
The A record is made, but the PTR fails.

192
00:09:50.150 --> 00:09:52.150
Create the reverse zone first.

193
00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:54.800
Two: typing the Network ID backwards.

194
00:09:55.050 --> 00:09:56.250
Type 192.

195
00:09:56.500 --> 00:09:57.700
168.

196
00:09:57.950 --> 00:10:01.550
50 in normal order, and the wizard
reverses it.

197
00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:05.000
Three: building the secondary before SRV01
allows transfers.

198
00:10:05.250 --> 00:10:09.250
Fix the Zone Transfers tab, then run
Start-DnsServerZoneTransfer on SRV-CORE.

199
00:10:09.500 --> 00:10:11.500
Four: adding records on SRV-CORE.

200
00:10:11.750 --> 00:10:13.350
The secondary is read-only.

201
00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:18.800
Make every change on SRV01, and let the
serial number carry it across.

202
00:10:19.050 --> 00:10:20.650
Time to check yourself.

203
00:10:20.900 --> 00:10:22.100
SRV01 holds lakeshore.

204
00:10:22.350 --> 00:10:26.350
test and the reverse zone, and SRV-CORE
holds a secondary.

205
00:10:26.600 --> 00:10:31.800
First, why is Store the zone in Active
Directory greyed out on SRV01?

206
00:10:32.050 --> 00:10:33.650
Second, NAS01 is 192.

207
00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:35.100
168.

208
00:10:35.350 --> 00:10:36.550
50.

209
00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:38.000
40.

210
00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:43.450
In which zone is its PTR record, and what
is the record's name?

211
00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:45.700
Third, what does Resolve-DnsName www.

212
00:10:45.950 --> 00:10:47.150
lakeshore.

213
00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:49.800
test return, and why two records?

214
00:10:50.050 --> 00:10:53.250
And fourth, you add a record on SRV01.

215
00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:58.700
How does SRV-CORE find out, and what
happens if the notify is lost?

216
00:10:58.950 --> 00:11:00.550
Pause the video now.

217
00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:02.400
Here are the answers.

218
00:11:02.650 --> 00:11:05.450
One: SRV01 is not a domain controller.

219
00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:10.100
Active Directory-integrated zones need a
writeable DC running DNS, so until

220
00:11:10.350 --> 00:11:13.150
Learning Plan 4 our zones are file-backed.

221
00:11:13.400 --> 00:11:16.200
Two: the PTR record is in 50.

222
00:11:16.450 --> 00:11:17.650
168.

223
00:11:17.900 --> 00:11:19.100
192.

224
00:11:19.350 --> 00:11:20.550
in-addr.

225
00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:22.000
arpa, named 40.

226
00:11:22.250 --> 00:11:28.650
Three: you get the CNAME, then the A
record, because www is an alias for srv01.

227
00:11:28.900 --> 00:11:32.900
Four: the serial goes up and SRV01 sends a
notify.

228
00:11:33.150 --> 00:11:38.750
If the notify is lost, SRV-CORE still
checks at the refresh interval, every 15

229
00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:40.200
minutes by default.

230
00:11:40.450 --> 00:11:44.050
On SRV01, I install DNS and open DNS
Manager.

231
00:11:44.300 --> 00:11:45.900
The wizard creates lakeshore.

232
00:11:46.150 --> 00:11:51.350
test as a file-backed primary with no
dynamic updates, then the reverse zone,

233
00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:52.800
Network ID 192.

234
00:11:53.050 --> 00:11:54.250
168.

235
00:11:54.500 --> 00:11:55.700
50.

236
00:11:55.950 --> 00:12:02.350
I add srv01 with the PTR box checked, dc01
with dash CreatePtr, then mail without a

237
00:12:02.600 --> 00:12:05.800
PTR, the CNAME www and the MX record.

238
00:12:06.050 --> 00:12:08.450
I allow transfers only to 192.

239
00:12:08.700 --> 00:12:09.900
168.

240
00:12:10.150 --> 00:12:11.350
50.

241
00:12:11.600 --> 00:12:12.800
21.

242
00:12:13.050 --> 00:12:15.850
On SRV-CORE I add the secondary zone.

243
00:12:16.100 --> 00:12:19.300
One new record on SRV01, and SRV-CORE
follows.

244
00:12:19.550 --> 00:12:20.750
To wrap up.

245
00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:27.400
The primary zone is writable, a secondary
is a read-only copy, and a stub only lists

246
00:12:27.650 --> 00:12:28.850
name servers.

247
00:12:29.100 --> 00:12:30.700
Our zones are file-backed.

248
00:12:30.950 --> 00:12:35.350
For a reverse zone, type the Network ID in
normal order.

249
00:12:35.600 --> 00:12:41.600
The PTR check box, or dash CreatePtr,
makes A and PTR records in one step.

250
00:12:41.850 --> 00:12:44.250
Allow zone transfers only to 192.

251
00:12:44.500 --> 00:12:45.700
168.

252
00:12:45.950 --> 00:12:47.150
50.

253
00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:51.000
21; the serial number and notify move each
change.

